==========================================
SYSADMIN DAY 08 NOTES — auditd
Date: 23-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- auditd is the kernel's "who did what" recorder; events go to /var/log/audit/audit.log
- A watch rule (-w path -p wa -k key) logs writes/attribute changes to one file; a syscall rule (-a always,exit -S ...)
  logs every use of a system call, which is powerful but very noisy
- -p letters: r read, w write, x execute, a attribute change; -k gives the rule a searchable label
- ausearch -k <key> -i finds events by label; aureport gives summaries
- uid is who the process ran as; auid is who really logged in - sudo changes uid but not auid
- auditctl rules live in memory only; permanent ones go in /etc/audit/rules.d/ and load with augenrules --load
- auditd rotates its own log (max_log_file / num_logs in auditd.conf)
- Never auditctl -e 2 casually: it locks the audit config until reboot

COMMANDS / ACTIONS I PRACTICED:
[ ] install + enable auditd, auditctl -s and -l
[ ] auditctl -w on my own test file, then a write and a chmod
[ ] ausearch -k lab-secret -i to read the events
[ ] writing to the file via sudo and comparing uid vs auid
[ ] watching execution of whoami (-p x)
[ ] aureport --summary / -k --summary
[ ] checking auditd.conf log rotation settings
[ ] making a rule permanent with rules.d + augenrules, then cleaning up

paste or describe what you saw :
